// Lets a plain-language request ("write me a letter to my landlord as a PDF")
// produce an actual downloadable document — the client (ChatBox.tsx) detects
// the ```pdf_doc fence and renders a download button; nothing here changes
// for any other reply. Used identically whether the conversation is in the
// dedicated pdf_maker domain, has the PDF plugin activated, or is spending
// its one free sample document (see app/api/chat/route.ts) — the model
// doesn't need to know which case it is, only that it's currently allowed to
// generate one. Distinct from RESUME_PDF_SUFFIX below, which is specific to
// the resume domain's own fixed name/experience/education schema.
export function generalPdfPrompt(): string {
  return (
    "\n\nIMPORTANT: This product has a real, working capability to generate actual downloadable PDF documents — it is not a generic text-based AI limitation here. If the user merely asks whether you can create/generate/export a PDF (a yes/no or capability question, not yet a request for specific content), answer yes and briefly say you can, without the usual disclaimers about being unable to produce files, and without yet emitting the block described below. " +
    "Before generating a brand-new document for the first time in this conversation (not a revision of one already generated), check whether the user already told you — in this message or earlier — whether they want a border and page numbers on the PDF. If either is missing, don't generate yet: ask ONE short, friendly question covering whatever's missing, and mention these are also available anytime as toggles in this document's settings (the \"Style\" button near the message box) if they'd rather set them there. Ask at most once per new document: once you've asked, or if the reply is vague (\"you choose\", \"doesn't matter\", \"just make it\"), generate immediately and don't ask again — a border and page numbers are otherwise applied automatically from the document's current settings when the user downloads it, so you never need to track the answer yourself or mention it again after this one check. A follow-up asking to change an already-generated document's content is a revision, not a new request, and should never re-ask this. " +
    'If, and only if, the user explicitly asks you to generate, finalize, or export an actual document as a PDF (a letter, report, proposal, memo, meeting notes, or similar — not a resume, which uses its own format elsewhere), do two things: give your normal reply as usual, and after it, include exactly one fenced code block tagged ```pdf_doc containing valid JSON of this shape: {"title":"...","subtitle":"...","sections":[{"heading":"...","paragraphs":["..."],"bullets":["..."],"numbered":["..."],"table":{"headers":["...","..."],"rows":[["...","..."],["...","..."]]},"chart":{"type":"bar","title":"...","data":[{"label":"...","value":0}]},"callout":{"kind":"note","text":"..."},"columns":{"left":{"heading":"...","paragraphs":["..."],"bullets":["..."]},"right":{"heading":"...","paragraphs":["..."],"bullets":["..."]}},"newPage":false}]}. ' +
    '"title" is required; everything else is optional — omit "subtitle" or any section field the content doesn\'t call for, rather than inventing filler. A section can mix these fields as needed, but most sections use just one or two of them. Use "bullets" for an unordered list, "numbered" when the order itself matters (steps, a ranked list) — never both for the same list. Use "table" only for genuinely tabular content (a schedule, a spec sheet, a simple invoice line-item list); "headers" is optional, every row must have the same number of cells as the headers (or match each other if there are no headers). ' +
    'Use "chart" only for genuinely numeric, categorical data worth visualizing — "type" is "bar" (comparing several categories) or "pie" (parts of a whole); "data" needs 2-8 entries, each a "label" and a numeric "value". It renders as static, server-drawn artwork in a small fixed set of grays — not an interactive or colored chart, so never mention colors when describing it. ' +
    'Use "callout" for a single short aside that should visually stand out from the surrounding text — a warning, a key takeaway, an important caveat — not for routine content; "text" is one short passage (not a list), "kind" is "note" (default), "warning", or "important" and only changes emphasis, never an actual color. ' +
    'Use "columns" for exactly two independent, parallel blocks of content meant to sit side by side (e.g. two options being compared, "before" and "after", two unrelated short topics) — not for a single flowing narrative, which stays as ordinary paragraphs. Keep each column short: a long column starts the whole block on a fresh page rather than letting the two sides split unevenly. ' +
    'Math is fully supported wherever text appears — "paragraphs", "bullets", "numbered", callout "text", table cells/headers, and column "paragraphs"/"bullets" — using standard LaTeX syntax: $...$ for inline math, $$...$$ for a centered display equation on its own line. Use it for genuine mathematical notation (equations, formulas, variables with subscripts/superscripts, Greek letters) rather than plain-text approximations, but never for chart data-point labels, which render as plain text. ' +
    "This produces a plain black-and-white PDF — never suggest actual colors, images, or custom fonts for it. \"columns\" is a layout choice, not a color choice, and a chart's shading is applied automatically from a small fixed set of grays — you never pick or specify a hex value or a colored chart. Border and page numbers are the only whole-document layout choices available, and both are applied at download time from the document's settings, never something you draw into the content yourself. " +
    'Every section may also carry "newPage":true to force it onto a fresh page — the ONLY reliable way to control page count, since it\'s a real, unconditional page break, not a hint the layout engine might ignore. It has no effect on the very first section (already on page 1); never set it there. ' +
    "If the user names a target page count (\"make it 15 pages\", \"a 5-page summary\"), treat that as a real length instruction, not a stylistic hint, and hit it deterministically the same way an exact slide count comes from emitting exactly that many entries in a deck's \"slides\" array: split the content into exactly N page-groups of one or more sections each, and put \"newPage\":true on the first section of every group after the first (N-1 forced breaks for N pages). This guarantees at least N pages outright — a forced break always starts a new page no matter how little precedes it — so the only remaining failure mode is a group's content overflowing onto an extra page, not falling short of the count. " +
    "Keep each page-group from overflowing by sizing its content to what one A4 page actually holds (10.5pt body text, 1.4 line-height, ~0.69in margins): roughly 700-800 words of plain paragraph text fill a page (about 50 lines) — noticeably more than it looks like from screen reading. Headings, bullet/numbered lists, and section spacing eat into that a bit without adding to the word count, and a table/chart/callout takes up its own space on top of it, so a group carrying one of those needs correspondingly less prose. Write fully developed paragraphs for each group, not brief summaries — expanding with more detail, sub-points, and supporting content rather than covering the same ground more tersely, and never stopping early just because every topic the user named has been touched on at least briefly. " +
    "Never emit this block for a message that's just discussing or brainstorming content, and never use it for a resume (use the resume domain's own format for that)."
  );
}

